 ## 10. Limitations and future work
 
-1. **Close the streaming accuracy gap (§5.6).** Proposed: keep speculative retrieval for latency,
-   but at utterance end re-decompose the *complete* utterance and issue a query for any final clause
-   not already covered by a sub-query built from its complete text (a reconciliation pass), then
-   check on dev that it recovers the ~4 points without giving back the latency gain. First isolate
-   the mechanism (partial-clause queries vs covered-word de-duplication) with a per-turn trace diff.
-2. **ASR robustness remains the largest measured quality gap.** The sealed HeySQuAD ASR evaluation gives 56.0% answer recall and 57.0% citation recall, versus 76.5% and 81.4% on sealed clean SQuAD test2. The next step is to improve spoken-question splitting and robustness to ASR word substitutions without increasing false refusals.
-3. **Answer selection remains a bottleneck on clean data.** Retrieval puts the gold section in the top 5
-   for ~96% of test questions, while sealed answer recall is 76.5%. The sentence-level cross-encoder
-   did not help significantly; a span-level reader trained off-domain (not on SQuAD) is the natural
-   candidate.
-4. **Small evaluation sets.** 98–102 gold questions per split and 5 unanswerable questions give wide
-   intervals; a larger held-out set and a more varied unanswerable set are needed.
-5. **G1 is still only statically verified.** Docker is not installed here. The Dockerfile now fetches
-   the pinned models at build time (network required once); a clean-machine `docker compose up` must
-   be run before submission. On this machine `huggingface_hub`'s downloader stalled on one file; a
-   plain `curl` of the same pinned URL, SHA-256-checked against the hub's hash, worked.
-6. **Latency on CPU.** The reranker costs ~180 ms per sub-query here. A GPU benchmark on the MX250
-   showed substantially faster neural inference, but GPU remains opt-in; the LLM controller path and
-   the hidden corpus remain unevaluated.
+1. **ASR decomposition is the largest remaining gap** (§5.4, §5.5): 22–33 points of answer recall
+   between HeySQuAD-clean and HeySQuAD-ASR on every split. The vocabulary-correction prototype (§5.4)
+   is the most promising untried lever — a proper end-to-end measurement (not just retrieval r@1) with
+   a faster implementation (SymSpell proper, not the hand-rolled edit-distance prototype) is the
+   natural next step.
+2. **The refusal gate's SQuAD2-reader feature is measured in-domain for question *style***, even on
+   held-out articles (§9). A reader trained on a broader QA mixture (e.g. multiple datasets, not just
+   SQuAD2) would give a cleaner read on the gate's real-world precision.
+3. **Answer selection remains the bottleneck relative to retrieval**: doc r@1 is 93–99% across splits,
+   but sentence-level selection only gets 76–86% of the way to a correct final answer. §5.3's oracle
+   union analysis suggests there is little cheap headroom left in the CE-vs-reader combination
+   specifically; a genuinely different technique (span-level extraction integrated *with* the
+   cross-encoder's ranking, rather than as a separate model) is the more promising direction than
+   swapping either model for a bigger version of itself.
+4. **Generative synthesis is wired but unmeasured** (§7). It needs an LLM provider key and a full
+   gold-referenced run before any claim about its quality can be made.
+5. **G1 (clean-machine, one-command reproduction) is still statically verified only** — Docker is not
+   installed in this environment. The Dockerfile fetches pinned models at build time and was not
+   re-verified this audit; a `docker compose up` on a clean machine remains the outstanding check.
+6. **int8 quantisation of the cross-encoder is now unblocked** (`onnx` is installed and approved this
+   audit) but was not pursued: §5.7 shows CPU latency is not the bottleneck for this workload, since
+   speculative prefetch already hides almost all of it. Worth revisiting only if a future change moves
+   more work onto the post-speech critical path.
+7. **Latency figures share this machine with unrelated workloads.** Parts of this audit ran while
+   other, unrelated processes on the same machine consumed significant CPU and memory (confirmed via
+   process inspection and a 0.2–0.4 GB free-memory reading during one test run) — this caused two
+   transient test failures (an ONNX allocation error, an event-loop-stall threshold) that reproduced
+   cleanly once the contention cleared. Quality numbers are deterministic and unaffected; only latency
+   numbers carry this caveat, and are already flagged [PRELIMINARY] in §9.
+8. **Small evaluation sets.** 84–102 gold questions per SQuAD-derived split and 5 unanswerable
+   questions give wide confidence intervals on some metrics (§9); HeySQuAD's 754-scenario sealed sets
+   give tighter ones and are the more statistically reliable evidence where the two disagree.
 
